DCE V1.3 for OpenVMS VAX and Alpha

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Includes a new utility, the "Resource Broker," which adds intelligence to
   application server deployment

o  Provides increased platform integration with an integrated login
   facility, and X.500 support

o  Includes new APIs and object-oriented Remote Procedure Call support,
   which extend the programming capabilities of DCE

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Digital's DCE (Distributed Computing Environment) V1.3 for the OpenVMS
product family is an implementation of the Open Software Foundation's (OSF)
DCE R1.0.3, adapted and enhanced for OpenVMS systems. V1.3 includes several
new features:

o  Resource Broker -- Resource Broker is an out-of-the-box monitoring and
   service access tool. When deployed within a DCE cell, Resource Broker
   determines the services and resources in a computing environment and
   makes them available to users via an easy-to-use graphical interface.
   With Resource Broker as the agent, users can find DCE servers across
   their cell and see usage information displayed in the form of a bar
   graph. Resource Broker is a valuable tool for isolating performance
   bottlenecks, such as computer overloads. With its unique cell-wide view
   of servers and resources, it provides dynamic, customizable server
   selection algorithms that can take into account availability,
   appropriateness, and efficiency, and can then choose to either utilize an
   existing server or start up a new one. This dynamic server selection
   capability is made available in the graphical user interface, command
   line interface, and also transparently via the standard Remote Procedure
   Call (RPC) Name Service Interface (NSI). As a result, the random-order
   server selection algorithm provided by the DCE standard specification can
   be replaced with user-defined load balancing algorithms without modifying
   the application.

o  Integrated login -- New in V1.3 is the integration of the DCE login with
   the OpenVMS login. Users can now log into OpenVMS and DCE environments
   with a single easy step. Administrators now have two new associated
   utilities; the DCE IMPORT and EXPORT utilities. The DCE IMPORT utility
   allows administrators to create principal and account entries in a DCE
   registry based on accounts in an existing OpenVMS authorization file. The
   DCE EXPORT utility enables administrators to create an OpenVMS
   authorization file based on accounts in an existing DCE registry.

o  Object-Oriented RPC -- The DCE Interface Definition Language (IDL)
   compiler has been extended to support a number of C++ language syntax
   features that provide a distributed object framework. The DCE RPC runtime
   environment now supports C++ bindings to remote objects. The combination
   of these new features creates an object-oriented RPC.

o  Generic Security Service API (GSSAPI) -- GSSAPI provides an application
   programming interface (API) that lets users employ DCE security to
   distribute applications that handle network communications by themselves.

o  X/Open XDS API and X.500 support -- V1.3 provides the XDS directory
   services API and also provides support so that the DCE Global Directory
   Agent (GDA) can utilize the X.500 protocol. The GDA provides a means of
   linking multiple Cell Directory Server namespaces via either the Internet
   Domain Name Server (BIND) or X.500.

BENEFITS

o  DCE for OpenVMS software allows programmers and administrators to develop
   and manage distributed applications that interoperate seamlessly in
   multivendor environments as easily as traditional, single-system
   programs.

o  Digital has the most comprehensive DCE strategy in the industry; it
   supports DCE on OpenVMS, Digital UNIX, and Windows systems (Windows NT
   version will be available in the future).

o  The combination of the DCE and OpenVMS feature rich, high integrity
   computing environment is ideal for client/server computing. When the
   price/performance of the Alpha platform is added, DCE and OpenVMS
   software make an excellent combination for distributed computing.

o  DCE Runtime Services are licensed free with the OpenVMS operating system
   at no additional cost; OpenVMS software comes "DCE-ready."

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The DCE V1.3 for OpenVMS product family (SPD 43.05.03) is available now
through Digital and its DECdirect service. The DCE software consists of
several layered products, each licensed separately to provide users with
maximum flexibility.

The right to use the DCE Runtime Services on OpenVMS systems is included
with the OpenVMS operating system license; no PAK is required. The media,
which contains binaries, is not yet shipped with OpenVMS software and must
be ordered separately.

Due to export restrictions, DCE V1.3 for OpenVMS software is not currently
available as part of the OpenVMS Consolidated Software Distribution (CONDIS)
CD-ROM. However, with an impending change to CONDIS CD-ROM export code,
Digital expects DCE to ship on CONDIS CD-ROM later in 1995. DCE V1.3 for
OpenVMS Alpha binaries will also ship on the CD-ROM containing the next
release of the OpenVMS Alpha operating system.

Documentation kits are the same for both the VAX and Alpha platforms.
QA-01RAA-GZ contains documentation for the DCE Runtime Services, DCE Cell
Directory Server, and DCE Security Server. QA-24CAA-GZ contains all the
documentation in QA-01RAA-GZ plus three additional books on DCE application
development.

