OpenVMS Alpha and VAX V6.2

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Support for new Alpha and VAX systems

o  Low cost, high availability SCSI-based clusters

o  PC-based OpenVMS Management Station software for simplified management
   across multiple clusters

o  OpenVMS Freeware CD-ROM and one OpenVMS documentation set for all
   architectures

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Digital announces the availability of OpenVMS Alpha V6.2 and OpenVMS VAX
V6.2. With the introduction of V6.2, the OpenVMS AXP operating system name
has been changed to the OpenVMS Alpha operating system.

The OpenVMS operating system runs on multiple platforms -- Alpha and VAX
systems. Digital's Alpha systems, the world's highest performance RISC
architecture computers, provide a smooth growth path for Digital's highly
successful line of CISC architecture VAX computers. The OpenVMS Alpha and
VAX systems operate side by side seamlessly. They connect to the same
networks and clusters, and share the same software, data, and applications.

OpenVMS systems are richly featured, high-integrity, commercial-strength
operating systems for secure, mission-critical computing. With OpenVMS
systems, users can deploy data and applications to meet the complete
computing requirements of their business. Users can be confident that their
computing environment provides unmatched functionality, robustness, and the
ability to deliver their business results.

The OpenVMS operating system supports both development and production
environments, and can be tuned easily to support all styles of computing
(e.g., client/server, interactive, batch, online transaction processing, and
computationally intensive environments). Furthermore, OpenVMS V6.2 supports
a complete range of systems, from Alpha based PC systems to large data
center systems.

Digital's implementation of cluster technology on OpenVMS systems is unique
in the industry. OpenVMS clusters present virtually unlimited growth
options. Only OpenVMS systems offer this premier clustering capability and
have enhanced it to include both Digital's VAX and Alpha processors. Many
users have already expanded their OpenVMS cluster environments by adding
Alpha systems, which allows them to continue sharing resources across
systems as well as across architectures. With OpenVMS Alpha V6.2, Digital
now introduces SCSI clusters, which provide low-cost, high-availability
entry systems for business critical servers.

The following new features are among those introduced in OpenVMS Alpha V6.2
and OpenVMS VAX V6.2. These features are new to both Alpha and VAX systems
unless otherwise indicted:

o  Low cost, high availability SCSI clusters are available on selected
   OpenVMS Alpha systems. OpenVMS Cluster Software V6.2 supports the ability
   to configure up to two CPUs on a common SCSI bus. This capability allows
   the configuration of entry-level, dual-host SCSI clusters on low-end
   AlphaServer systems that complement traditional DSSI clusters on Alpha
   and VAX departmental servers, and CI clusters on Alpha and VAX enterprise
   servers. (See the "SCSI Clusters For OpenVMS V6.2" article in this
   issue.)

o  This release offers support for the following systems:

   Alpha Systems
   - AlphaServer 8200 5/300
   - AlphaServer 8400 5/300
   - AlphaServer 2100 5/250

   VAX Systems
   - VAXstation 4000-96
   - MicroVAX 3100-96
   - VAX4000-106A

   The three new VAX systems listed above will work with OpenVMS VAX
   V5.5-2H4, 6.1 and 6.2. They will not work with OpenVMS V6.0.

   OpenVMS Alpha V6.2 reintegrates support for systems currently supported
   by OpenVMS Alpha V6.1-1H1 and V6.1-1H2.

o  The OpenVMS Management Station software enables management of one or more
   OpenVMS cluster systems from a single Windows based PC. This software is
   based on PATHWORKS ManageWORKS V5.0, which presents a hierarchical view
   and provides the ability to manage multiple OpenVMS systems from a single
   point of control. It enables users to view, organize, and manage objects
   in a way that is meaningful to them. This is the first version of the
   Management Station software and provides user account management and the
   infrastructure for future functionality.

o  Integration and ease of use between OpenVMS and TCP/IP products has been
   improved, providing TCP/IP vendor neutral interoperability.

o  OpenVMS V6.2 is Internet ready. Included in this release are all the
   tools and utilities that enable users to become part of the explosive
   growth on the Internet. The National Center for Supercomputer
   Applications (NCSA) Mosaic web browser is included in OpenVMS V6.2 (on
   the DECwindows Motif V1.2-3 distribution media). A complete suite of
   Internet utilities is included on the Freeware CD-ROM.

o  Functional equivalence enhancements that improve the interchangeability
   and migration between VAX and Alpha systems have been added:

   - SCSI-2 ANSI standard support has been added to OpenVMS Alpha systems,
     providing tagged command queuing and other SCSI-2 industry standard
     features. This support greatly improves the speed of high performance
     industry standard SCSI storage devices on Alpha workstations and
     servers. It also provides support for SCSI-2 fast wide differential
     host adapters, 16 bit devices, and RAID array controllers.

   - DECnet/OSI Fullnames support has been added to the OpenVMS Alpha
     operating system. This feature was introduced with OpenVMS VAX V6.1 and
     provides underlying support for network protocols using hierarchical
     naming, such as DECnet/OSI and TCP/IP. It also allows the use of node
     names up to 255 characters in length, both interactively and within
     applications.

   - Dynamic device recognition has been added to OpenVMS VAX systems,
     allowing devices to "plug and run" -- no operating system upgrades are
     needed.

   - Command recall has been enhanced on OpenVMS VAX V6.2, and now allows up
     to 254 commands in the command recall stack and a command recall file.

   - Many general-purpose DCL enhancements requested by users have been
     added to OpenVMS V6.2.

   - Additional security system services simplify the creation of robust
     server applications.

o  OpenVMS Cluster enhancements:

   - Clusterwide SCSI tape serving is now supported by OpenVMS systems. The
     OpenVMS tape mass storage control protocol allows locally connected
     SCSI tape drives to be accessible to all members of an OpenVMS Cluster.

   - New OpenVMS Cluster Client Software (SPD 42.18.xx) provides a low-cost
     way to add Alpha and VAX workstations to an OpenVMS Cluster. OpenVMS
     Cluster Client Software provides full function clustering with the
     following exceptions - (1) Client nodes may not provide votes to the
     cluster, and (2) Client nodes may not serve disks or tapes to other
     nodes in the cluster. OpenVMS Cluster Client Software is included in
     the NAS150 product.

   - Multi-site clusters allow nodes in an OpenVMS Cluster to be linked
     using FDDI bridges in conjunction with ATM and DS3/T3 lines over
     distances of up to 150 miles.

   - The requirement to configure all OpenVMS Cluster systems with a DECnet
     license has been removed in OpenVMS V6.2. OpenVMS Clusters are fully
     supported in TCP/IP-only environments. As part of the engineering
     development to permit this enhancement, a completely new OpenVMS
     Cluster satellite booting mechanism is provided in OpenVMS V6.2. Most
     users are expected to continue to use the existing DECnet mechanisms,
     while the new satellite booting feature is suitable for environments
     without DECnet. This new feature will also provide MOP downline load
     service for any device (such as terminal servers and LAN-based
     printers).

   - DECamds software has been enhanced to support an Alpha-based console.
     Previously, the DECamds console was restricted to VAX systems only.

   - The OpenVMS V6.2 operating system provides enhanced failover
     capabilities for OpenVMS clusters. It is now possible to connect a pair
     of HSC/HSJ controllers to separate star couplers. This feature provides
     increased availability for the disks that are dual ported across the
     pair of controllers, because the failure of a CI adapter does not
     impact access to the disks. This feature is also supported for pairs of
     HSD30 controllers configured across two DSSI rails.

o  Volume Shadowing enhancements:

   - In response to user demand, the limits have been increased for the
     number of shadow sets allowed per cluster. Each cluster can now support
     an unlimited number of single member shadow sets, and up to 400 disks
     in multi-member shadow sets (i.e., 200 two-member or 133 three-member
     shadow sets).

   - Geometry-based device recognition is supported for determining device
     inclusion in shadow sets. Volume Shadowing no longer checks device
     identifications, but instead checks device geometry and LBNs to
     determine if two devices can be members of the same shadow set. Devices
     like the RZ28 and RZ28B can now operate in the same shadow set.

   - StorageWorks RAID Array 210 and 230 subsystems can now have their
     attached SCSI disks included in Volume Shadowing shadow sets.

o  The new automatic foreign command feature provides the ability for users
   to define a command search path for both images and command procedures.
   This is equivalent to the path mechanism on UNIX and DOS systems.

o  A new front-end interface to the backup utility enables occasional backup
   users and untrained system managers to easily and efficiently perform
   backup operations.

o  New OpenVMS packaging:

   - The OpenVMS Freeware V1.0 CD-ROM is included in the OpenVMS
     distribution kit. The CD-ROM includes Digital internal tools such as
     SDL, NMAIL, MAILWATCH, and popular Internet programs.

   - There is now one OpenVMS documentation set for Alpha and VAX systems.
     Users can order the base or full documentation set for the OpenVMS
     operating system, which is common for both Alpha and VAX systems.
     CD-ROM users will receive the full OpenVMS documentation set in
     Bookreader format.

   - Easier installation and upgrades of key products is now possible with
     the inclusion of POSIX and DCE Runtime on the OpenVMS operating system
     binary CD-ROM. Also included on the operating system binary CD-ROM are
     DECwindows Motif (with Mosaic client), TCP/IP Services, DECmigrate,
     SoftPC and SoftWindows, and the DCE Application Developers Kit, DCE
     Cell Directory Server, and DCE Security Server. These products are part
     of the PCSI installation routine on OpenVMS Alpha systems and are
     separately installable using PCSI or VMSINSTAL kits on OpenVMS VAX
     systems. Except for POSIX and DCE, all other layered products require a
     separate license and product authorization key (PAK).

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The following are the upgrade paths for OpenVMS Alpha and VAX users:

OpenVMS Alpha systems:
   OpenVMS Alpha V6.1 to V6.2
   OpenVMS Alpha V6.1-1H1 to V6.2
   OpenVMS Alpha V6.1-1H2 to V6.2
   OpenVMS Alpha V1.5 to V6.1 to V6.2

OpenVMS VAX systems:
   OpenVMS VAX V6.1 to OpenVMS VAX V6.2
   OpenVMS VAX V6.0 to OpenVMS VAX V6.2
   OpenVMS VAX V5.5-2H4 to V6.1 to V6.2
   OpenVMS VAX V5.5-2 to V6.0 to V6.2
   OpenVMS VAX V5.5-2 to V6.1 to V6.2

All applications running on OpenVMS Alpha V6.1 will continue to run on
OpenVMS Alpha V6.2, and applications running on OpenVMS VAX V6.1 will
continue to run on OpenVMS VAX V6.2.

OpenVMS Alpha V6.2 media and documentation can be ordered separately.
OpenVMS Alpha binaries are distributed on compact disc. The OpenVMS
documentation is included in the kit on a separate disk in online Bookreader
format. Users desiring hardcopy documentation must order it separately.

OpenVMS VAX V6.2 binaries are distributed on compact disc, TK50, and
magtape. Hardcopy documentation is included as part of the distribution kit.

Systems shipping with factory-installed software will be delivered with
OpenVMS Alpha V6.2 or OpenVMS VAX V6.2 pre-installed.

