RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia V3.0

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Mainframe access from a Multia Desktop

o  Support for graphics on mainframe included

o  Provides access to mainframe via TN3270 and Microsoft SNA server

o  Included on Multia System Administrator Kit as optional software

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia V3.0 is a software product, layered on Multia
Base Software V3.0, that allows a Multia Desktop connected in a TCP/IP or
NetWare environment to access IBM mainframe-based applications
interactively. Access to IBM mainframe applications is gained via TN3270
sessions either via gateways on the mainframe or through Microsoft's SNA
server.

In normal operation, an IBM application transfers a formatted screen much
like a form or panel to the 3270 display, and the user fills in any data
required by the form. When complete, the form is transmitted to the IBM host
system in block mode (as a single unit) rather than in character mode (a
single character at a time). This mechanism is emulated within a Multia
system by buffering the form in memory on the Multia system and presenting
it on the video screen. When the form is complete (the user has filled in
all the relevant information), it is sent to the IBM system via the
specified SNA connection.

RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia software is the result of a porting effort of
the "best in class" technology from the Wall Data Corporation. It operates
as a native mode, 32 bit Windows NT application under Microsoft Windows NT
V3.5 and takes advantage of the Windows NT virtual memory management system.
Since the various elements of RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia are brought
into main Multia memory only when they are required, and only the minimum
core set of modules remain resident at any given time, the number of
sessions supported is limited only by the memory size of the Multia system.
RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia requires 4.8 MB of hard disk space.

Users invoke RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia software by clicking on a RUMBA
for Mainframe for Multia icon under Windows NT or via the setup in the
Multia Applications Manager. Initially, RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia
software may be configured by selecting the FastPath menu entry, which
guides the user through the configuration and connection process, creating a
RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia profile. This profile may be saved and
invoked for future use so that the configuration process does not have to be
repeated each time the IBM mainframe application is accessed. A profile may
be created for each mainframe application that is used, and each profile may
be customized by the user for keyboard layout, color schemes, screen sizes,
Hotspots, QuickSteps, macros, and more.

RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia software emulates the following 3270 display
stations: 3278/79, 3178/79, 3179-G, 3192-G, and 3472-G. All popular terminal
models (2 through 5) and screen sizes (1920, 2560, 3440, and 3564
characters) are supported. With its auto font control feature, RUMBA for
Mainframe automatically senses the model and screen size from the initial
information sent by the IBM host computer and adjusts the character fonts
accordingly. While sessions are running, the session window may be sized at
will by the user, and the character fonts will be sized within the window.
Support for True Type fonts is included, permitting better text visibility
on high resolution monitors.

The software includes the graphics option APA (all points addressable,
vector graphics). Mainframe graphics applications can be presented at the
Multia Desktop. RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia software supports
Telegraf/Teleplan, Interactive Chart Utility, GDDM, and other APA graphics
applications.

This product also allows the user to select the keyboard layout that
corresponds to the Multia's keyboard. The most popular standard and enhanced
keyboard configurations are supported and are menu selectable. The Keyboard
Mapping function provides the ability to add, delete, and modify key
combinations to suit user needs. The customized configuration may be saved
as part of the profile for the specific application session. A graphical
on-screen keyboard layout may be invoked at any time for quick reference of
key assignments. Certain national language keyboard character sets are
supported as well.

RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia software's custom color capability enables
the user to choose the color for every facet of the 3270 application screen.
Virtually unlimited hues, brightness, and color intensity may be assigned to
background, foreground, characters, and host attributes to create a
distinctive application screen presentation.

Its QuickStep macro function is a colorful, easy way to bypass memorization
and keying of complex, repetitious, and tedious keystroke sequences.
QuickStep allows the user to assign such command and keystroke sequences to
a single key. QuickStep also enables the user to create macros for
automating functions such as opening other Windows applications, launching
file transfers, and host log-on and log-off commands. Macros can be listed
by file name or by description. With QuickStep, the assigned keys are
displayed graphically on a customized on-screen keypad. The keypad may
contain as many keys as the user desires and may be identified with names,
colors, and highlighting. A click of the mouse on a QuickStep keypad key
causes the function to be immediately executed automatically. As with the
keyboard layout and screen color definitions, the QuickStep keypads may be
saved with the application's profile for use whenever the host application
session is activated. The QuickStep pads can be resized or minimized to help
reduce screen clutter.

The software's macro system also includes the Hotspots function, which can
be used to activate macros by pointing and clicking the mouse on special
text on the screen. Certain keys and commands are used regularly in the
screen text of 3270 applications (such as PA and PF keys). RUMBA software
includes a set of these function keys and commands as default Hotspots that
will perform the appropriate action when clicked with the mouse. The user
has the ability to create custom Hotspots for any application and to save
them as part of the session profile.

Transferring of files between host mainframe and the Multia Desktop is as
simple as a few clicks of the mouse using the Transfer Menu function. The
transfer program uses IBM's standard IND$FILE for 3270 data stream transfer
to and from TSO, CMS, and CICS host environments. In addition, DISOSS file
transfer is supported. Multiple background file transfers may be activated
concurrently with foreground interactive sessions.

There is also the capability to cut and paste data between two applications,
when one set of data is on the Multia system and the other is on the
mainframe, both sets are on the Multia device, or both sets are on the
mainframe. The Hotlinks feature enables automatic data exchanges between a
Windows application on the Multia system (such as Microsoft's Excel for
Windows) and the mainframe application. Whenever data on the mainframe
changes, it is updated in the Multia system's Windows application
automatically.

RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia software supports a floating tool bar, which
puts the most frequently used commands on the button bar that appears at the
top of the RUMBA session window. The tool bar can be moved, repositioned, or
resized within the RUMBA software window. Text descriptions of each button
can be toggled on or off in the 3D status bar at the bottom of the screen.
The 3D status bar provides greater visibility for system status information.

The software emulates an IBM 3287 printer when a printer session is active.
Configuring printer sessions is performed by mouse point-and-click
selections on a graphical printer control panel screen. As with all other
functions of RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia, the printer sessions may be
extensively customized to the user's needs and desires. RUMBA for Mainframe
for Multia software supports all the Microsoft Windows printer drivers,
including PostScript printers. Locally attached printers, as well as those
shared on a LAN, are supported.

Online, context-sensitive help is always available to the user at the click
of a mouse button or by typing Ctrl+H. Information is instantly available
about the topic or feature desired -- what it does and how to use it. Help
also contains a graphical introduction to RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia
features, and a tutorial on their usage.

Supported Protocols

A Multia Desktop running RUMBA for Mainframe for Multia software can connect
to a mainframe in three ways:

o  Connecting to an industry standard gateway supporting TN3270 sessions via
   TCP/IP, such as Novell NetWare for SAA Version 2.0

o  Connecting to a Microsoft SNA server

o  Connecting to a front-end processor on a mainframe supporting TN3270
   sessions

