SCSI Clusters for OpenVMS V6.2

HIGHLIGHTS

o  OpenVMS V6.2 introduces a shared-storage cluster interconnect -- SCSI
   Clusters

o  The world's best clusters are now available at a lower cost

o  Supports low-end and mid-range PCI-based Alpha systems

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Users want the high availability and world leadership functionality of
OpenVMS Clusters at a lower cost than previously available, using industry
standard SCSI devices with convenient packaging. OpenVMS V6.2 SCSI Clusters
are evidence of Digital's long-term commitment to significantly enhance
OpenVMS Clusters for mission critical applications into the next century.

Prior to V6.2, OpenVMS Clusters implemented shared storage subsystems using
the CI and DSSI interconnects. SCSI disks could be connected to these buses
using HSJ/HSC and HSD series controllers. With V6.2, each of two SCSI buses
can be configured with up to two CPUs and six shared disks. This removes the
requirement to configure HS family controllers, providing a lower entry cost
for OpenVMS Clusters. A generalized schematic view of a SCSI Cluster is
shown below:

   	   (Disk)    (Disk) 	(Disk)	 (Disk)	   (Disk)    (Disk)
   	      |	     	|   	  |   	    |  	      |	     	|
   	|-----+-----+---+---------+---------+---------+--+------+---|
   	      	    |	    	  SCSI Bus     	      	 |
   	     +------+-------+	      	       	 +-------+------+
   	     |	AlphaServer |	      	       	 | AlphaServer	|
   	     |	    or	    |	OpenVMS Cluster	 |    or     	|
   	     | AlphaStation |	    Server     	 | AlphaStation	|
   	     |	     	    |	      	       	 |    	     	|
   	     +------+-------+	      	       	 +-------+------+
   	      	    |	    	      	       	      	 |
   	      	    |	    LAN (Ethernet or FDDI)    	 |
   <<---------------+------------------------------------+---------------->>
   	   |  	     	|   	    | 	       |      	 |   	     |
   	   |  	     	|   	    | 	       |      	 |   	     |
   	<client>     <client>	<client>    <client>  <client>	 <client>

SCSI Cluster Features

o  Direct connection of SCSI disks and two Alpha CPUs are provided to a
   fully shared SCSI bus.

o  Both CPUs can access the disks at the same time. This is superior to UNIX
   multi-host SCSI offerings, in which only one host can access a disk at a
   time (the other host providing a "hot-standby" mode of operation).

o  Up to two shared buses are supported -- permitting 12 shared disks,
   providing over 24 GB of shared data. (More disks may be configured if
   HSZ40 controllers are included in the configuration.)

o  Fully redundant (no single point of failure) configurations are possible.

o  Rackmount configurations are orderable on a single part number.

BENEFITS

OpenVMS SCSI Clusters allow users to configure high availability OpenVMS
Cluster server configurations at a lower cost than ever before. By directly
sharing storage and CPUs on the same SCSI bus, a separate storage controller
such as the HSD30 is not required. For small configurations (with up to 12
disks), this results in significant cost savings, while providing full
strength OpenVMS Cluster capability.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

SCSI Clusters for OpenVMS V6.2 is available now through Digital and its
business partners. SCSI Cluster support is provided as an integral part of
OpenVMS Cluster software for OpenVMS V6.2. No additional license is
necessary. Every node in an OpenVMS Cluster must be separately licensed. (An
OpenVMS Cluster software license is included in every NAS 300 and NAS 400
product, which ships with many server configurations.)

Some of the more common hardware required to configure an OpenVMS SCSI
Cluster is listed below. Several additional items may also be required, such
as cables and terminators, depending on the specific configuration. Fully
configured rackmounted OpenVMS SCSI Clusters also are available.

