TECHNICAL DATA

Hardware Supported

OpenVMS V6.2 SCSI Clusters support the following hardware:

CPUs:	 AlphaServer 2100 4/xxx
	 AlphaServer 2000 4/xxx
	 AlphaServer 1000 4/xxx
	 AlphaServer 400 4/xxx
	 AlphaStation 400 4/xxx
	 AlphaStation 250 4/xxx
	 AlphaStation 200 4/xxx

No Alpha systems with the new Alpha 21164 microprocessor are supported for
use as SCSI Cluster systems. Support for these systems will be in the
future.

SCSI adapters: 	The embedded adapter included in all supported systems.
                Optional add-on KZPAA adapters. (For ease of configuration,
                usage of these adapters is strongly recommended.)

Disks:		RZ26, RZ26L, RZ28, RZ28B, RZ29B

Converters:	DWZZA (converts single-ended SCSI to Fast Wide Differential
                SCSI)

Controllers:	HSZ40

Several other SCSI adapters, such as the KZPSA, are supported by OpenVMS
V6.2 as single-host controllers for both standalone and clustered systems.
However, in OpenVMS V6.2, they are not supported for use with more than one
CPU (multiple hosts) attached to the SCSI bus.

Additional system, adapter, and disk support will become available in future
releases of OpenVMS software.

Configuration Rules

The following rules apply to SCSI Cluster configurations:

o  A maximum of two CPUs may be configured on a SCSI bus. Any mix of
   supported system models is valid.
o  A CPU may be connected to a maximum of two shared SCSI buses.
o  A second separate interconnect must also be configured between the two
   CPUs -- this may be a LAN (Ethernet or FDDI) or DSSI. The interconnect is
   required for OpenVMS Cluster SCSI communications (such as the Lock
   Manager).
o  Only disks may be configured on a multi-host SCSI bus. Tapes, floppies,
   and CD-ROMs must be configured on a separate single host bus. Usually, a
   KZPAA adapter is used to connect to shared buses, while the system local
   embedded adapter is used for tapes, floppies and CD-ROMs.
o  Non Digital disks may be configured on a multi-host bus provided they
   adhere to the appropriate SCSI specifications. Refer to the OpenVMS V6.2
   New Features Manual (AA-QCM1A-RE) for further details. Areas of concern
   include multi-host support, tagged command queueing, and bad-block
   revectoring.
o  Systems configured on a multi-host SCSI bus may also be part of a larger
   OpenVMS Cluster configuration. Refer to the OpenVMS Cluster SPD (42.18)
   for additional details.
o  General SCSI bus configuration rules must be obeyed. These define maximum
   bus lengths and termination rules:

   -  The maximum length of a slow single-ended SCSI bus is 6 meters.
   -  The maximum length of a fast single-ended SCSI bus is 3 meters.
   -  The maximum length of a fast differential SCSI bus is 25 meters.
   -  A SCSI bus may be configured with a maximum of two terminators, and
      they must be located at the physical ends of the bus.

Configurations

There are many possible configurations of OpenVMS SCSI Clusters, covering a
range of performance, scalability, availability, and cost. For detailed
configuration information, please refer to the OpenVMS V6.2 New Features
Manual (AA-QCM1A-RE). This manual comprehensively describes how to configure
an OpenVMS SCSI Cluster.

The following examples show the rear view of three possible OpenVMS SCSI
Cluster configurations. The pictures show typical physical layout and
cabling.

Example 1

Shows the cabling layout for a configuration based on AlphaServer 1000
systems, with KZPAA adapters and a StorageWorks BA350 enclosure. Another
pair of KZPAA adapters with an additional BA350 could be added to this
configuration easily, thereby doubling the total storage capacity.

(Type COPY SALES::SNUFFY$:[SU_LIB]2706_*.PS/LOG *.* at prompt to obtain
PostScript versions of this issue's art from the Sales Update Library. For
this article's art only, type the above command and include "SCSI_CLUS"
before the "*.PS".) <artwork 2706_SCSI_CLUS2.PS>

Example 2

Shows the cabling layout for a configuration that includes a StorageWorks
BA350 disk enclosure and an HSZ40 controller. The DWZZA signal converters
are necessary to provide additional SCSI cable length, and the Fast Wide
Differential signaling that is required by the HSZ40 controller.

(Type COPY SALES::SNUFFY$:[SU_LIB]2706_*.PS/LOG *.* at prompt to obtain
PostScript versions of this issue's art from the Sales Update Library. For
this article's art only, type the above command and include "SCSI_CLUS"
before the "*.PS".) <artwork 2706_SCSI_CLUS3.PS>

Example 3

Shows the cabling layout for the simplest type of SCSI Cluster -- comprising
two AlphaStation systems directly connected, using the system integrated
SCSI adapters. Storage for this configuration is internal to the two
systems. This type of configuration results in the lowest possible cost.

Because this configuration uses the system integrated SCSI adapters to
connect to the multi-host SCSI bus, there is no single-host SCSI bus on
which to configure tapes, floppies, or CD-ROMs.

Digital suggests that optional KZPAA adapters are always used to connect to
multi-host SCSI buses. When this process is done, the system integrated SCSI
adapter can be used for tapes, floppies, CD-ROMs, and unshared disks (such
as local page/swapping disks). When KZPAA adapters are used to connect to a
multi-host SCSI bus, storage is external to the systems. In this example, a
BA353 "pizza box" StorageWorks box would be used to hold the shared disks.
This results in higher availability because the Alpha systems can be shut
off without removing power from the disks (this is not possible when the
disks are internal to the systems).

(Type COPY SALES::SNUFFY$:[SU_LIB]2706_*.PS/LOG *.* at prompt to obtain
PostScript versions of this issue's art from the Sales Update Library. For
this article's art only, type the above command and include "SCSI_CLUS"
before the "*.PS".) <artwork 2706_SCSI_CLUS4.PS>

Operating System Support

SCSI Clusters are supported by OpenVMS Alpha V6.2, using the hardware
described above. With OpenVMS V6.2, SCSI Clusters do not support DEC 2000
Models 300/500, DEC 3000, DEC 4000, DEC 7000 systems or any VAX systems.

